CONVOLVE
++++++++

SUMMARY
-------
Compute the convolution of a signal with all time series in memory.

SYNTAX
------
::

  CONVOLVE  {FILENAME} {MASTER n} {BOX v} {TRI v} {GAUSS v} {MAG v} {TRAP v1 v2}

INPUT
-----

   :MASTER n: Convolve with filenumber n

   :FILENAME: Convolve with data in FILENAME

   :BOX v: Convolve with a box of width v seconds

   :TRI v: Convolve with a triangle of half width v seconds

   :GAUSS v: Convolve with a gaussian with standard deviation v seconds

   :MAG v: Convolve with a triangle of half width with desired magnitude v

   :TRAP v1 v2: Convolve with a trapezoid of rise v1 and width v2

   :CENTERED ON|OFF: Place zero time on maximum amplitude or at beginning of pulse

DEFAULT VALUES
--------------
::

  CONVOLVE MASTER 1 CENTERED ON

DESCRIPTION
-----------
  Assuming the first time-series file in memory is a pulse or response
  file or ::

    CONVOLVE MASTER 1

  The first file is convolved with all of the time-series files in
  memory using the discretized version of the following equation::

      conv(tau) = int ( f(t) g(tau - t) ) dt,

  where f is the first file, g any from among the other signals in
  memory, and int means summation over all times t, or integration.

  INPUT DELTA: All signals in memory must have the same DELTA.

  INPUT NPTS: The number of points in f, the pulse shape, cannot be
  greater than the number of points in any other file in memory.

  OUTPUT NPTS: If npts(g) is the number of points in g and npts(f) the
  number in f, the number in conv is npts(g) + npts(f) - 1.

  OUTPUT TIMING: The start time for each output conv will be the same
  as that for the input g.

PREDEFINED FUNCTIONS
--------------------
  The option to convolve with BOX, TRI, GAUSS, MAG, or TRAP
  automatically computes these function with the correct time
  samplings.

  TIMING/CENTERED: The maximum amplitude CENTERED at zero time.  For the BOX
  function, the ZERO time is defined as the half-width.  Centering on
  the pulse shapes eliminates time shifts introduced by convolution.
  If CENTERED is OFF, then the first data point of the pulses is
  defined as zero.

  AMPLITUDE: All Functions are normalized such that they integrate to
  1.0 and should not add amplitude, or "moment" in the language of
  sources, to the final, convolved signal.

  MAGNITUDE: The MAG option uses the relationship between triangle
  half width and magnitude defined in Wells and Coppersmith, 1994,
  Table 2A, All::

     M = a + b * log(SRL)

  where a = 5.08, b = 1.16, and assumes a rupture velocity, vr, of
  2.88 km/s or vr ~ 0.8 vs where vs, 3.6 km/s, is the shear wave speed
  at the fault; See Lay and Wallace, Sec 9.3, p. 379.  Large magnitude
  will produce extremely wide triangle and these events are probably
  better represenated with a BOX or a TRAP.


RELATION TO CORRELATE
---------------------
  Note the similarity in the convolve equation and the one in CORRELATE_.
  Many correlate routines, including the original in SAC, mistakenly
  use this similarity in deriving the procedure for convolution by simply
  perturbing the procedure used in CORRELATE. The time series from a
  convolution (conv in the equation above) is typically a replacement time
  series for the time series g; for example, adding a pulse shape or an
  instrument response to a synthetic seismogram.  The relative times for f
  and g are not used in CORRELATE_, but in convolution they may play a role.

  For example, g might be a synthetic time series of spikes with
  time offsets, amplitudes, and polarities representing phase arrivals
  The synthetic may have Gibbs phenomena accompanying each arrival.
  A way to improve the appearance is to convolve g with a time-symmetric
  triangle function, e.g. ::

     SAC> fg triangle npts 8 delta 0.02 begin -0.08

  If one did not take into account the start time of f, the arrivals in conv
  would be offset by 0.08 seconds.  A script showing runs with f given by
  both that triangle pulse and also a pulse shape starting at time zero
  is included in ${SACHOME}/doc/examples/convolve.

